INTRODUCTION
Environmental fluctuations significantly challenge gas monitoring, particularly when measuring trace gases in the atmos
phere.1−3 This challenge is compounded for NDIR sensors, which, despite their high sensitivity and selectivity for specific gas molecules, are highly susceptible to dynamic environmental
conditions.4,5 However, their low cost and easy-to-maintain structures also provide broad prospects for their application under such conditions. Compared to the more established NDIR technology for CO2 monitoring, CH4 monitoring presents additional challenges due to its significantly lower atmospheric concentration, weaker infrared absorption bands, and higher susceptibility to cross-interference from other
gases.6−8 Therefore, accurately analyzing the response characteristics of NDIR technology to CH4 under various environmental conditions and exploring methods to enhance its monitoring capabilities are of significant scientific and practical importance. To address the challenges posed by environmental fluctuations, recent advancements in NDIR sensor technology have focused on modeling sensor behavior with consideration of ambient temperature, relative humidity, measured re
sponses, and concentration levels as key inputs.9,10 Enhancements in environmental corrections and calibration techniques
have substantially improved sensor stability and accuracy, particularly in applications related to automotive and environ
mental monitoring.4,11,12 While these innovations have driven significant advancements in CO2 sensing, the development of similarly precise and commercially viable NDIR sensors for CH4 monitoring remains challenging, particularly under dynamic environmental conditions. For CH4 monitoring, environmental corrections often require advanced modeling approaches to address the nonlinear impacts of temperature, as well as the need to distinguish CH4 signals from overlapping absorption features
of H2O and CO2.6,13 The dynamic nature of environmental conditions continues to introduce biases and variability, necessitating the integration of advanced computational methods, such as machine learning and data assimilation, to
effectively mitigate these challenges.14−19 An NDIR CO2 sensor with an XGBoost-based drift compensation model
