Cross-Influence of Temperature and Humidity. Based on simulations using the HITRAN database, which provides high-resolution molecular absorption data, reported in our
previous study,36 H2O molecules are likely a significant factor affecting the NDIR analysis of CH4 gas. To investigate this, we conducted a cross-influence analysis within an environmental temperature range of 10−40 °C, with increments of 10 °C, and a relative humidity range of 10−70%, with increments of 20%. Under these varying conditions, we observed the response trends of a gas detector for CH4 concentrations ranging from 5 to 500 ppm. The signal response trends are shown in Figure 3a−d, where the top surface represents baseline reference signals measured from ambient air without added CH4, and the bottom surface represents variations under different relative humidity conditions for distinct CH4 concentrations introduced into the experimental setup. At an ambient temperature of 10 °C, the signal response reaches its minimum at a relative humidity of approximately 50%. As the temperature increases to 20 °C, this minimum shifts to around 30% humidity. When the temperature reaches 30 °C, the minimum signal response occurs at humidities of roughly 10% and 50%. By 40 °C, the minimum temperature is observed at 70% humidity. This phenomenon likely arises from the complex interaction of water vapor molecules with CH4, including absorption band interference and scattering effects, which modulate the infrared signal received by the detector. The highest response values are consistently seen in high humidity environments, particularly around 70%, while the minimum response values shift with temperature, reflecting the varying influence of H2O molecules on the sensor’s performance. To assess the response limits in the low concentration range of 5−25 ppm of CH4, the response ratio between reference and target signals was calculated using eq 1. As shown in Figure S5, at temperatures below 20 °C, the signal stability is
poor across all humidity levels, and resolution between CH4 concentrations is low. At 30 °C and 10% humidity, the response ratio resolution improves, achieving a detection limit of 5 ppm. However, increasing humidity reduces resolution, raising the detection limit to 15−25 ppm. At 40 °C, excellent resolution and a 5 ppm detection limit are achieved at 10% and 30% humidity, but higher humidity again diminishes resolution, pushing the detection limit to 15−25 ppm. Thus, the combined effect of temperature and humidity significantly impacts the CH4 detection limit, with optimal performance observed at 40 °C and a humidity below 30%.
