Field Test Insights on CH4 Baseline Dynamics. During a 21-h field test conducted on April 27−28, 2024, at the INAS site, we carried out a comprehensive baseline survey to better understand the local environmental conditions. This baseline survey, conducted at a site with no prior reports of CH4 emissions, provided an ideal setting for characterizing the
influence of real-world environmental factors on CH4 monitoring and was critical for interpreting the sensor data. The lack of CH4 emissions can likely be attributed to the absence of wetlands or permafrost in the area, and the vegetation within 100 m of the test point, primarily consisting of Solidago altissima, Zoysia grass, Kudzu, and Hotei-chiku, has not been associated with significant CH4 production in previous studies, as shown in Figure 5a. Moreover, the physical and chemical properties of the soil were also investigated, with a detailed explanation provided in Table S5. The observed variations in CO2 concentration, chamber temperature, and soil salinity reveal a strong positive correlation between CO2 and temperature, while CO2 shows a negative correlation with salinity, as shown in Figure 5b. A notable finding was that changes in CO2 concentration lagged behind temperature fluctuations by approximately 2 h, likely due to delayed responses from plant photosynthesis and soil microbial activity. Nighttime showed more pronounced CO2 fluctuations compared to those of daytime, indicating increased plant respiration emissions during the night. These findings underscore the dynamic nature of CO2 concentrations in field conditions, which is crucial for understanding their potential impact on CH4 detection, especially when compared to
